Subject: fix(run): scope the minted token on forges that are not github.com

A run that mints a narrowly scoped token keeps calling the forge with the
ambient credential it was launched with, on any GitHub Enterprise Server
install. The log shows the mint succeeding, the request carries the wrong
identity, and nothing distinguishes the two.

gh chooses which environment variable it reads a credential from by host:
GH_TOKEN and GITHUB_TOKEN for github.com, GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN and
GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN for everything else. mintAgentToken sets only
GH_TOKEN, so off github.com it writes to a variable gh will not read, and
whatever the environment already carries in GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN stays in
charge for the sandbox stream, the post-script, and every host_files
expansion that follows.

Set both, chosen from the host, and restore both in the existing cleanup.
syncRunnerEnvTokens refreshes them too, so a remint does not leave the first
mint's token snapshotted in RunnerEnv (the same bug as #7231, one variable
over).

The host comes from GH_HOST, falling back to GITHUB_SERVER_URL. GH_HOST is
consulted first because gh itself prefers it. On github.com the function
returns nothing and behaviour is unchanged.

Found against a GitHub API emulator serving a private host, where a status
comment posted by the minted bot identity sat next to an agent comment posted
as the run actor, which is what made the split visible.

Breadboard note: patches Go source, so it belongs to the build patch list in
deploy/scripts/05i-build-fullsend.sh rather than the mirror list. Written to
be sent upstream as-is. Add a DCO sign-off before submitting it as a
human-authored change.

